; The ALIAS-FLATTENED spelling of a same-chain nested `.cbor` payload — `inner = bytes .cbor uint`
; referenced as `bytes .cbor inner` — in top-level rule-BODY position. Rejected gracefully, with
; the rule name in the message (the body seam is the only one that has one).
;
; This is the SAME encoding chain as the inline sibling
; (tests/robustness/cbor_payload_same_chain_inline.cddl), not a lookalike: `inner`'s own `.cbor`
; lives in its `RustType`'s ENCODING list, and resolving a reference to `inner` copies that type
; whole, so by the time the operation applies `inner` and the parenthesized inline form both carry
; one `CBORBytes` and the second application lands on a chain that already has one. Naming the
; payload adds no boundary — which is a property of the encoding list, not of the conceptual `Alias`
; node (the member/arm seam keeps that node, and the refusal fires there just the same).
; A refusal keyed on the inline SPELLING would therefore miss this one entirely, which is why the
; key is the chain rather than the syntax — and why a multi-link alias chain
; (tests/robustness/cbor_ref_alias_chain.cddl's depth axis) is covered by the same key with no
; extra case.
;
; Rule-BODY position is deliberate for the second reason as well: it lowers to a `pub type` alias
; that mints no serializer, so this spelling generated a crate that BUILT while being exactly as
; unsupported as the member one. The refusal is on the composition, so the two agree.
inner = bytes .cbor uint
b = bytes .cbor inner
